<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as RSS, National Volunteer Union</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh (RSS) is an Indian right-wing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a> volunteer paramilitary organisation, founded on 27 September 1925 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> by K. B. Hedgewar, a doctor influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinayak-damodar-savarkar">Vinayak Damodar Savarkar</a>'s Essentials of Hindutva. It set out to unite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> community and establish a Hindu Rashtra, and has been accused of intolerance of minorities, particularly Muslims. It played no role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> movement. It was banned in 1948 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nathuram-godse">Nathuram Godse</a>, a member, assassinated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>, again during the Emergency and after the demolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babri-masjid">Babri Masjid</a> in 1992. It leads the Sangh Parivar, which includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharatiya-janata-party">Bharatiya Janata Party</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rashtriya-swayamsevak-sangh.jpg" alt="Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suyash Dwivedi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Path_Sanchalan_Bhopal-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>K. B. Hedgewar, a doctor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a>, founded the Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh in 1925, to propagate the ideology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a> and give what it called new physical strength to the majority community. Its initial aim was character training and '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> discipline' in order to unite the Hindu community and establish a Hindu Rashtra, a Hindu nation. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Tilak</a> died in 1920, Hedgewar and other followers of Tilak in Nagpur parted ways with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>, whose stance on the Khilafat Movement concerned them and whose Congress did not take up cow protection. Hedgewar was jailed for a year in 1921 on a charge of sedition.</p><p>The 1920s saw Hindu-Muslim relations deteriorate, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mappila">Moplah</a> rebellion of August 1921 and riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> in 1923. Christophe Jaffrelot describes a theme of stigmatisation and emulation: Muslims, Christians and the British were treated as foreign bodies, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> nationalists copied what they saw as the source of their strength, such as paramilitary organisation and unity. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-mahasabha">Hindu Mahasabha</a> was an important influence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The organisation kept apart from political organisations then fighting British rule, and it did not take part in the independence movement against British rule. During the Partition it helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> refugees fleeing West Punjab, and its activists also took part in communal violence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-india">North India</a>. It was banned briefly in Punjab Province in January 1947, and after independence three times: in 1948, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nathuram-godse">Nathuram Godse</a>, a member, assassinated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>; during the Emergency of 1975 to 1977; and after the demolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babri-masjid">Babri Masjid</a> in 1992.</p><p>In a letter of December 1947 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> called it a private army proceeding on the strictest Nazi lines. It at first did not recognise the national flag or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india">Constitution of India</a>, and its magazine Organiser criticised the Constitution on 30 November 1949 for not mentioning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manusmriti">Manusmriti</a>. In 1954 its volunteers took part in the removal of Portuguese rule from Dadra and Nagar Haveli.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The RSS has no formal membership. Men and boys join by attending the nearest shakha, or branch, which is the basic unit, and women are not allowed to join. It is the progenitor and leader of a large body of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a> organisations, the Sangh Parivar, which includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharatiya-janata-party">Bharatiya Janata Party</a>. Its affiliated organisations have set up numerous schools, charities and clubs. Its supporters describe an aim of upholding Indian culture and its civilisational values, while critics accuse it of intolerance of minorities, particularly Muslims.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>B. R. Ambedkar insisted that freedom must end caste oppression; the Hindu Mahasabha and the <b>Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh</b>, founded in 1925, promoted Hindu nationalism; and most princes, who met in the Chamber of Princes from 1921, wished to keep their thrones, while popular movements in their states demanded…</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Savarkar’s ideology reached K. B. Hedgewar in Nagpur in 1925; after discussions with Savarkar he founded the <b>Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh</b> in September of that year, a paramilitary organisation whose shakhas held physical and martial training and ideological lessons.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/hindutva">Hindutva · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
